@author "Tincture authors"
@$VER: Tincture.guide 1.0 (08.11.2026)
@wordwrap
@node main "Tincture"

                        @{b}Tincture 1.0@{ub}
              A paint program for AmigaOS 4.1

Tincture paints in true colour or with a palette of up to 256 colours,
in layers, with unlimited undo (as far as memory goes), and speaks
ARexx. It is written in Boing BASIC.

    @{" The window      " link window}   tool box, picture, colours, layers
    @{" Tools           " link tools}   what each tool does, and its key
    @{" Colours         " link colours}   pens, the picker, indexed pictures
    @{" Layers          " link layers}   transparency, opacity, merging
    @{" Selections      " link selection}   cut, copy, paste, move, brushes
    @{" Files           " link files}   IFF ILBM, PNG, .tinc, drag and drop
    @{" Menus           " link menus}   every menu item and its Amiga key
    @{" Keys            " link keys}   all the keyboard shortcuts
    @{" ARexx           " link arexx}   scripting Tincture
    @{" Settings        " link settings}   preferences and autosave
    @{" Starting        " link starting}   command line and tooltypes

@endnode

@node window "The window"
@{b}The window@{ub}

@{u}Tool box@{uu} (left): the twelve tools, then Undo and Redo. The tool in use
has an amber frame.

@{u}Picture@{uu} (middle, it grows with the window): paint with the left mouse button in the foreground
colour, with the right button (or Alt and the left button) in the
background colour. The menus open with the right button anywhere else. Drag with the
middle button, or hold Space and drag, to move the picture around.
The mouse wheel scrolls; with Ctrl, or when zoomed in, it zooms around
the pointer. The outline under the pointer shows the brush.

With the @{" magnifier " link menus} on, the right part of the picture area
shows the place under the pointer magnified, with a pixel grid. You
can paint in both parts.

@{u}Colour panel@{uu} (top right): the foreground pen over the background pen
(click to swap them), the palette, and a colour picker: a square of
saturation and brightness for the hue chosen in the bar below it.

@{u}Brush and layers@{uu} (right): brush size (1 to 64) and shape, then the
layer list with the top layer first, buttons for new, delete, up and
down, and the current layer's visibility and opacity.

@{u}Status line@{uu} (bottom): pointer position, picture size, zoom, tool,
selection size, layer, colour mode and the last message.
@endnode

@node tools "Tools"
@{b}Tools@{ub}

  Freehand          d   paints with the brush; with a custom brush,
                        stamps it along the stroke
  Line              v   Shift: 0, 45 or 90 degrees
  Rectangle         r   outline in the brush size; Shift: square
  Filled rectangle  R
  Ellipse           o   inside the dragged box; Shift: circle
  Filled ellipse    O
  Flood fill        f   fills the area of one colour in the layer
  Airbrush          a   sprays while the button is held
  Text              t   asks for the text, then click to place it;
                        it floats until you click outside it
  Select            s   drag a rectangle; drag inside it to move its
                        contents, Ctrl+drag to move a copy
  Pick colour       i   takes the colour under the pointer (right
                        button: for the background pen)
  Eraser            e   paints transparency on upper layers and the
                        background colour on the bottom layer

Every stroke, fill or shape is one step of @{" undo " link menus}.
@endnode

@node colours "Colours"
@{b}Colours@{ub}

Click a palette pen for the foreground colour, right-click for the
background colour. The picker sets the foreground colour directly.

To change a pen, Shift-click it (or double-click the palette): the
picker then edits that pen, shown with an amber frame. Esc stops
editing.

@{b}True colour@{ub} pictures (the default) use any colour; the palette is
just a set of handy pens and is kept in the settings.

@{b}Indexed@{ub} pictures (Picture > Indexed Colours...) have at most 256
colours, the palette's. Converting reduces the picture's colours with
a median cut, with dithering if Picture > Dither when Reducing is on.
In an indexed picture, changing a pen changes every pixel painted with
it, and the picker always gives the nearest pen. IFF ILBM and PNG
files are saved with the palette (1 to 8 bitplanes).
@endnode

@node layers "Layers"
@{b}Layers@{ub}

A picture has up to 16 layers. The bottom one is opaque; the others
are transparent where nothing was painted, so you can paint over a
picture without touching it. Each layer can be hidden and has an
opacity (0 to 255).

New, Delete, Up and Down are under the layer list and in the Layer
menu, with Merge Down (the current layer into the one below) and
Flatten (all into one). Double-click a layer to rename it.

Only the @{" .tinc " link files} format keeps the layers. Saving as IFF or
PNG saves the flattened picture.
@endnode

@node selection "Selections and brushes"
@{b}Selections and brushes@{ub}

Drag a rectangle with the Select tool (or Edit > Select All). The
selection shows as marching ants.

  Cut, Copy      put the selection of the current layer on the
                 clipboard
  Paste          the clipboard floats over the picture at the
                 pointer: drag it, then click outside to put it
                 down (Esc throws it away)
  Clear (Del)    empties the selection
  Crop           the picture becomes the selection

@{b}Custom brushes@{ub}: Edit > Brush from Selection makes the selection the
brush; its background-colour pixels are transparent. The Freehand
tool then stamps it. Brush in One Colour paints its shape in the
foreground colour. Flip (h), Rotate (z), Double and Half Size change
it; Built-in Brush goes back to the round brush.
@endnode

@node files "Files"
@{b}Files@{ub}

  IFF ILBM (.iff)   24-bit, or 1 to 8 bitplanes with the palette for
                    indexed pictures; reads HAM6/HAM8, EHB, masks
  PNG (.png)        truecolour, with alpha, or with a palette
  Tincture (.tinc)  all layers with names, visibility, opacity, the
                    palette and the colour mode

Tincture reads IFF and PNG itself (the same everywhere), and other
formats (JPEG, GIF, BMP, ...) through the datatypes.

Drop picture icons on the window, or on Tincture's icon on Workbench
(Settings > Icon on Workbench), to open them. If the picture has
changes or layers, the dropped picture is pasted as a floating
selection instead.

Files are written to a new file first and then renamed, so a crash
never leaves half a picture. With Settings > Create Icons, saved
pictures get an icon.
@endnode

@node menus "Menus"
@{b}Menus@{ub}

@{u}Project@{uu}  New... (A-N), Open... (A-O), Open as Layer..., Revert,
         Save (A-S), Save As... (A-A), Help..., About, Quit (A-Q)

@{u}Edit@{uu}     Undo (A-Z), Redo (A-Y), Cut (A-X), Copy (A-C), Paste (A-V),
         Clear (Del), Select All (A-L), Select None, Crop to
         Selection, Brush from Selection (A-B), Brush in One Colour,
         Flip Brush, Rotate Brush, Brush Double Size, Brush Half
         Size, Built-in Brush

@{u}Picture@{uu}  Canvas Size..., Scale..., Flip Horizontally, Flip
         Vertically, Rotate Right, Rotate Left, Indexed Colours...,
         Dither when Reducing, True Colour, Default Palette

@{u}Layer@{uu}    New Layer, Delete Layer, Move Up, Move Down, Merge Down,
         Flatten

@{u}View@{uu}     Zoom In (+), Zoom Out (-), Actual Size (1), Fit in
         Window (0), Magnifier (A-M), Pixel Grid (A-G), Symmetry
         (Off, Left and Right, Top and Bottom, Four Ways: Freehand,
         Airbrush and Eraser paint mirrored; the axes show dashed)

@{u}Settings@{uu} Autosave, Create Icons, Icon on Workbench (an AppIcon:
         drop pictures on it to open them), Save Settings
@endnode

@node keys "Keys"
@{b}Keys@{ub}

  d v r R o O f a t s i e   tools (see @{" Tools " link tools})
  x                         swap foreground and background pens
  [  ]                      brush smaller / bigger
  +  -  1  0                zoom in, out, 100%, fit
  m  g                      magnifier, pixel grid
  u  U                      undo, redo
  h  z                      flip / rotate the custom brush
  Cursor keys               scroll
  Space + drag              scroll with the mouse
  Esc                       cancel the stroke, the floating selection
                            or the pen editing
  Del                       clear the selection
  Help, F1                  this help
@endnode

@node arexx "ARexx"
@{b}ARexx@{ub}

Tincture's ARexx port is TINCTURE (TINCTURE.1 if a second Tincture
runs). Commands return their result in RESULT (use OPTIONS RESULTS);
errors set RC to 10 and RESULT to the message. Coordinates are
picture pixels; colours are "R G B" or "#RRGGBB". The same commands
work in a script file: Tincture --script file.

  NEW w h                   a new picture
  OPEN file / SAVE [file [PNG|ILBM|TINC]]
  INFO                      "w h layers current mode colours changed"
  COLOR r g b | #rrggbb     foreground pen (no argument: read it)
  BGCOLOR ...               background pen
  PALETTE i [colour]        read or set a pen
  PEN i                     foreground = pen i
  TOOL name                 FREEHAND LINE RECT RECTF ELLIPSE ELLIPSEF
                            FILL AIRBRUSH TEXT SELECT PICK ERASER
  BRUSH size [SQUARE]
  PLOT x y / DRAW x1 y1 x2 y2 ... / LINE x1 y1 x2 y2
  RECT x y w h [FILL] / BOX x y w h
  ELLIPSE cx cy rx ry [FILL] / CIRCLE cx cy r [FILL]
  FILL x y [tolerance] / SPRAY x y [radius [density]]
  ERASE x1 y1 x2 y2 ... / TEXT x y "text" [scale]
     (add BG to any of these to paint in the background colour)
  GETPIXEL x y              "#rrggbb" of the picture
  SELECT x y w h | ALL | NONE
  COPY / CUT / CLEAR / PASTE x y [FLOAT] / MOVE dx dy / DROP
  MAKEBRUSH / RESETBRUSH / BRUSHMODE OWN|COLOUR
  UNDO [n] / REDO [n] / UNDOCOUNT
  LAYER ADD [name] | DELETE | SELECT n | UP | DOWN | MERGE |
        FLATTEN | HIDE [n] | SHOW [n] | OPACITY n | NAME s
  FLIP H|V / ROTATE 90|180|270 / SCALE w h / CANVASSIZE w h
  CROP [x y w h]
  INDEXED n [DITHER] / TRUECOLOUR
  SYMMETRY OFF|H|V|4 / MAGNIFIER ON|OFF [x y] / GRID ON|OFF
  ZOOM [percent] / HASH / VERSION / QUIT

Example:

  /* sunset.rexx */
  OPTIONS RESULTS
  ADDRESS TINCTURE
  NEW 320 256
  COLOR 255 140 0
  CIRCLE 160 180 60 FILL
  COLOR 20 20 60
  BOX 0 180 320 76
  SAVE "RAM:sunset.iff"
@endnode

@node settings "Settings and autosave"
@{b}Settings@{ub}

Settings > Save Settings keeps the brush, the palette and pens, the
grid and magnifier, dithering, icons and the last drawer in
Tincture.prefs next to the program.

@{b}Autosave@{ub}: while Settings > Autosave is on, Tincture saves the
picture with all its layers every 30 seconds (the icon's tooltype
AUTOSAVE=seconds changes it, 0 turns it off) when it changed, into
Tincture.autosave.tinc next to the program. When Tincture ends
normally the file is deleted. If Tincture (or the machine) crashed,
the next start offers to bring the picture back.
@endnode

@node starting "Starting Tincture"
@{b}Starting Tincture@{ub}

From the Shell:

  Tincture [picture] [--size WxH] [--script file]

From Workbench: double-click the icon, or a picture's icon whose
default tool is Tincture, or drop pictures on the window.
@endnode
